# imwel core constraints

Full rationale: `AGENTS.md`. Keep this list short; do not duplicate that file.

## Product boundary

- imwel is an npm CLI only — no backend, database, hosted platform, or custom ACL/versioning that replaces Git / the Git host.
- Flag scope creep if a change reintroduces server-side accounts, phone-home governance, or a non-Git content store.

## Architecture

- Git is the database (blob/tree/commit SHAs). Do not invent parallel content hashes or changelogs when `git` already answers.
- The Git host is governance (permissions, PR/MR). Do not build access-control inside imwel.
- Bindings are per-directory (`.imwel/binding.yaml`), not per-repo monorepo magic.
- Rule artifacts: agents.md-flavored Markdown + `targetOverrides` at render time — no competing rule dialect.
- Local history = real hidden Git under `.imwel/history/`. Conflicts = standard markers; never silent auto-resolve.
- Drift checks: throttled / explicit CLI only — never hook an AI tool's session lifecycle.
- Upstream push defaults to branch + PR/MR; direct push only as explicit per-remote opt-in.
- Shell out to system `git` — no isomorphic-git or embedded Git library.

## Tech stack

- TypeScript on Node.js; single npm package for v1.
- One interactive-prompts library for CLI prompts — do not mix prompt libraries.
- Do not add monorepo tooling unless a second independently versioned deployable exists.

## Code & CLI strings

- All source identifiers, comments, commit messages, and code docstrings: **English**.
- User-facing CLI strings: never hardcode — use `src/locales/` (en default, zh-CN maintained).
- Scaffold files for *user* template repos live under `templates/init/<locale>/` — separate from CLI i18n.

## KISS / YAGNI

- Implement only what current specs/tasks require.
- Extract shared helpers only at the second real call site.
- Comments explain non-obvious *why*, never obvious *what*.

## Safety

- Do not write unmanaged files without explicit user action (`init` / confirmed `sync` / `propose`).
- Do not silently overwrite locally edited artifacts — detect drift first.
- No network fetch/push except explicit commands or throttled passive check — no daemons.

## CLI vocabulary

- Prefer established verbs: `init`, `sync`, `status`, `push`, `propose`, `rollback`, `remote`, `template` (and later roadmap verbs that fit this family).
- Multi-step / network commands must print progress; confirmations must state what will change; errors must be actionable.

## Roadmap note

- Template-repo AI authoring (Slash Commands, repo-type detection) belongs to `template-author-experience` — do not invent ad-hoc author UX in unrelated changes.
